#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98fb5d45-5215-506c-8b2a-99aaadb6631c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "3590f0e0-2ff9-58f4-8351-772b477c1cd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4109" :question/problem "If $\\log_7 (2x + 1) = 1$, then what is $x$?" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7$, so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{7} (2x + 1) &= 1 \\\\\n7^{\\log_{7} (2x + 1)} &= 7^{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 1 &= 7 \\\\\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b024b05-1efe-594e-9a7e-6d51a28c0c78" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7055ec96-b829-5cca-8149-314b4c8fc6c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5111" :question/problem "Given that $2\\log_5 (x-3)+1 = 5$, then what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\log_{5} (x - 3) + 1 &= 5 \\\\\n2\\log_{5} (x - 3) &= 4 \\\\\n\\log_{5} (x - 3) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5$, so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{5} (x - 3) &= 2 \\\\\n5^{\\log_{5} (x - 3)} &= 5^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3 &= 5^{2} \\\\\nx - 3 &= 25 \\\\\nx &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "77f6d553-3291-5110-a30e-11991ae88425" :question/math-academy-id "e-5118" :question/problem "If $\\log_2 x = 5$, then what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2$, so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} x &= 5 \\\\\n2^{\\log_{2} x} &= 2^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n2^{\\log_2 x} = x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5} \\\\\nx &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "310db608-6d8b-51af-8abd-60f4dbd05423" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "bd3c7932-a6eb-5de6-a485-84e5d44656a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5119" :question/problem "If $7\\log_6{x} - 3\\log_6{x} = 2$, then what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7\\log_{6} x - 3\\log_{6} x &= 2 \\\\\n(7 - 3)\\log_{6} x &= 2 \\\\\n4\\log_{6} x &= 2 \\\\\n\\log_{6} x &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6$, so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{6} x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n6^{\\log_{6} x} &= 6^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n6^{\\log_6 x} = x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6^{1/2} \\\\\nx &= \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "310db608-6d8b-51af-8abd-60f4dbd05423" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c83d3104-e874-508c-b1b2-d01d79f86eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-100631" :question/problem "If ${log}_{8}x+2{log}_{8}x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}x+2{log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee631ca-08b6-57a2-baf8-01c55b9afe77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059221} {:answer/id #uuid "c5af626c-0f95-5482-8308-f1c6ab338191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059222} {:answer/id #uuid "28ce4cae-57d3-5a1a-b387-e5356ff9eb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059223} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6093c7-d7d3-5b73-8456-ffe862656b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059224} {:answer/id #uuid "4a120f6a-8cf8-5a36-8599-d3bf2ba42ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059225}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dc9fc087-b031-5720-bd66-af06b92f0136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059220}] :question/id #uuid "c23322a3-0d32-5f5e-94fe-517b1c9adee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-906" :question/problem "If $\\log_{4} x = 3$, then $x =$"}]}
